{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790731094,"nonce":"H3RF5Rmjw8WOffb5D0qF2o93ZpI=","title":"Linked extern hooks hide dependencies added by their frontend bodies","message":"Split snapshot linking records each function ID after it has been followed. An external hook shell has no blocks, but `adopt_prelowered_reached` marked its ID as followed after adopting the shell. When a frontend later supplied a body under that same ID, the linker skipped the body and never adopted the functions it called.\n\nPython's `zb_hook_raise` follows this pattern: the builtins snapshot declares it as an external hook and the Python frontend supplies its implementation. The implementation boxes exception instances through `zb_box_instance`. Because that dependency was omitted, Python conformance failed across array, dict, exceptions, functions, inference, json, list, modules, set, tuple, and unary categories with `unknown function 'HirId(...)'`. The array module also lost type codes when related helpers were absent.\n\nDo not mark empty function shells as followed. Once a real body replaces the shell, scan it and adopt its linked dependencies. Add a split-snapshot regression covering a library entry that reaches an external hook whose frontend body reaches another library function.\n\nValidation:\n\n- `cargo test -p zyntax_compiler --test prelowered_linking`\n- `cargo test -p zyntax_python --test conformance modules -- --nocapture`: unknown-function and array regressions removed; only the independent `operator_functools.py` abort remains\n- Full Python conformance: failures reduced from 22 category lanes to 6 lanes across three independent categories; no `unknown function 'HirId(...)'` failures remain","files":null}]}